    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess in suspected juvenile idiopathic arthritis:\n- **Joint symptoms**: Number and pattern of involved joints, duration of symptoms (must be > 6 weeks for JIA diagnosis), morning stiffness duration (>30 minutes suggests inflammatory arthritis), gelling phenomenon (stiffness after prolonged sitting).\n- **Systemic features**: Fever pattern (quotidian spiking fevers suggest systemic JIA), rash (evanescent salmon-colored rash in systemic JIA), lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, serositis.\n- **Ocular symptoms**: Chronic anterior uveitis is a major complication of JIA (especially oligoarticular and ANA-positive). Often asymptomatic initially — ask about eye redness, pain, photophobia, visual changes. **Routine slit-lamp screening is mandatory**.\n- **Growth and development**: Assess height velocity — chronic inflammation and corticosteroid use can impair linear growth. Localized overgrowth (leg length discrepancy from asymmetric knee involvement) can also occur.\n- **Functional impact**: School attendance, physical activity, peer relationships, ADL difficulties (writing, dressing, stairs). Use the Childhood Health Assessment Questionnaire (CHAQ).\n- **Psychosocial**: Screen for depression, anxiety, and social isolation. Chronic illness in adolescents significantly impacts self-esteem and peer relationships.\n- **Red flags for malignancy**: Bone pain out of proportion, night sweats, weight loss, severe fatigue, unexplained cytopenias — must rule out leukemia before diagnosing JIA.',
