    const Section(
      title: '+ Classify the subtypes of JIA, identify the most likely subtype in this patient, and outline the differential diagnosis with appropriate laboratory workup.',
      content: '**ILAR Classification of JIA Subtypes** (International League of Associations for Rheumatology):\n\n1. **Oligoarticular JIA** (most common, approximately 50%): 1-4 joints in the first 6 months. Two subtypes: persistent (remains <=4 joints) and extended (spreads to >4 joints after 6 months). Peak age 2-4 years, female predominant, highest ANA positivity, highest risk for uveitis.\n\n2. **Polyarticular JIA, RF-negative** (approximately 20%): >=5 joints in first 6 months, RF negative. Bimodal age distribution (2-4 years and 6-12 years). Symmetric involvement common.\n\n3. **Polyarticular JIA, RF-positive** (approximately 5%): >=5 joints, RF positive. Essentially childhood-onset RA. Older girls (late childhood/adolescence). More aggressive course, erosive disease.\n\n4. **Systemic JIA (Still disease)** (approximately 10%): Quotidian (daily) spiking fevers plus arthritis. Evanescent salmon-colored rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, serositis. Equal sex distribution. Risk of macrophage activation syndrome (MAS).\n\n5. **Enthesitis-related arthritis** (approximately 10%): Arthritis plus enthesitis. Older boys. HLA-B27 associated. May progress to ankylosing spondylitis. Acute (not chronic) uveitis.\n\n6. **Psoriatic arthritis** (approximately 5%): Arthritis plus psoriasis or dactylitis/nail pitting plus family history.\n\n7. **Undifferentiated arthritis**: Does not fit one category or fits more than one.\n\n**This Patient**: Most likely **Polyarticular JIA, RF-negative or RF-positive** — she has >=5 joints involved (bilateral knees + bilateral wrists = 4 large joints, meeting polyarticular threshold if one more joint qualifies), 6-month duration, symmetric, adolescent female, family history of RA.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis**:\n1. **Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)**: Polyarthritis in an adolescent female. Check ANA, anti-dsDNA, complement levels (C3, C4), CBC, urinalysis for proteinuria.\n2. **Lyme disease**: Oligoarticular (especially knee), but