    const Section(
      title: '+ Design a comprehensive rehabilitation and medical management plan including exercise prescription, joint protection, and coordination with rheumatology for DMARD and biologic therapy.',
      content: '**Rehabilitation Program**:\n\n1. **Exercise Prescription** (critical and evidence-based in JIA):\n- **Aerobic exercise**: 30-45 minutes of moderate-intensity activity at least 5 days/week. Low-impact preferred: swimming (excellent — buoyancy reduces joint stress, warm water reduces stiffness), cycling, walking. Start at tolerated intensity and gradually increase.\n- **Strengthening**: Focus on quadriceps (protect the knee), hip stabilizers, and grip strength. Open-chain exercises initially if joints are inflamed (leg extensions, straight leg raises), progressing to closed-chain (squats, lunges) as inflammation is controlled.\n- **Flexibility**: Daily stretching, especially for hamstrings and quadriceps to address the developing knee flexion contracture. Prone lying for 20 minutes daily to stretch knee into extension.\n- **Key message**: Exercise does NOT damage joints in JIA. Children should be as active as possible. Deconditioning and muscle atrophy from inactivity worsen outcomes.\n\n2. **Joint Protection Strategies**:\n- Activity pacing: alternate periods of activity and rest during flares.\n- Wrist splints (resting splints at night if wrist involvement is symptomatic) to maintain alignment and reduce morning stiffness.\n- Proper backpack use (<=10% body weight, both straps).\n- Ergonomic school setup: supportive chair, appropriate desk height, keyboard/mouse positioning.\n\n3. **Physical Therapy**: Referral for 2-3x/week supervised therapy initially, then transition to a home exercise program. Focus on knee ROM restoration (address flexion contracture), quadriceps strengthening, gait normalization.\n\n4. **Occupational Therapy**: Assess hand function, adaptive devices if needed (built-up pen grips for writing), energy conservation education.\n\n**Pharmacologic Management** (coordinated with Pediatric Rheumatology):\n\n1. **NSAIDs** (first-line, symptom control): Naproxen 10-15 mg/kg/day divided BID (maximum 1000 mg/day). Common first NSAID for JIA due to BID dosing and good safety profile. Allow 4-6 weeks for full effect.\n\n2. **Intra-articular corticosteroid injection**: Triamcinolone hexacetonide (preferred in JIA — longer duration of action than acetonide) into the most symptomatic knee(s). Can provide months of relief and is a bridge while DMARDs take effect. May require sedation in a 12-year-old.\n\n3. **DMARDs** (disease-modifying, if insufficient response to NSAIDs within 2-3 months):\n- **Methotrexate**: First-line DMARD for polyarticular JIA. 10-15 mg/m2/week (subcutaneous preferred for better bioavailability). Requires folic acid supplementation, baseline and periodic monitoring of CBC, liver enzymes, and renal function.\n\n4. **Biologic therapy** (if inadequate response to methotrexate after 3-6 months):\n- **TNF inhibitors**: Etanercept (0.8 mg/kg/week SQ, max 50 mg) or adalimumab (24 mg/m2 every 2 weeks SQ). Well-studied in pediatric JIA with excellent efficacy.\n- **IL-6 inhibitor**: Tocilizumab (IV or SQ) — alternative if TNF inhibitor fails.\n- Requires screening for tuberculosis (PPD or QuantiFERON Gold) and hepatitis B before starting biologics.\n\n5. **Uveitis screening schedule**: ANA-positive polyarticular JIA requires slit-lamp exam every 3-6 months. Frequency depends on age, ANA status, and disease duration.',
