      content: '**School PE Modifications**:\n- The child should have a **504 plan** documenting her medical condition and PE modifications. She should NOT be excused from PE entirely — physical activity is therapeutic.\n- Modifications: excused from timed runs or high-impact activities during flares, allowed warm-up time for morning stiffness, permitted to self-pace activities, option to swim instead of run, ice packs available after class.\n- Communicate directly with the PE teacher (with parental consent) about JIA and activity guidelines.\n- Provide a written note specifying: \"May participate in modified PE. Avoid high-impact repetitive loading during active flares. Allow self-pacing and rest breaks as needed.\"\n\n**Safe Sports Participation**:\n- **Encouraged**: Swimming (best overall exercise for JIA), cycling, yoga, walking, elliptical, dance, martial arts (non-contact), golf.\n- **With caution/modification**: Soccer (her sport of choice) — can participate when disease is well-controlled on medication. Use appropriate bracing if needed. Monitor for flares after games. High-level competitive play may need to be modified during active disease.\n- **Generally avoid during active disease**: Sports with high joint impact (gymnastics tumbling, distance running on hard surfaces) or high collision risk (football, rugby, wrestling).\n- **Key message for this adolescent**: The goal of treatment is to allow her to return to the activities she loves. With modern JIA therapy (biologics), the majority of children achieve inactive disease or remission and can participate in sports.\n\n**Transition Planning (Pediatric to Adult Rheumatology)**:\nTransition planning should begin by age 12-14 and complete by 18-21:\n\n1. **Self-management education** (start now at age 12):\n- Teach the patient about her diagnosis, medications, and side effects.\n- Gradually shift responsibility from parent to patient for scheduling appointments, refilling medications, and recognizing flares.\n- Use the **Got Transition** program readiness assessments annually.\n\n2. **Healthcare literacy** (ages 14-16):\n- Introduce concepts of insurance, medication coverage, prior authorizations.\n- Teach her to communicate directly with her medical team.\n- Create a portable medical summary she can carry.\n\n3. **Transfer of care** (ages 16-21):\n- Identify an adult rheumatologist and schedule an overlap visit (joint appointment with pediatric and adult providers).\n- Transfer medical records, medication history, and treatment response.\n- **Critical risk period**: The transition gap between pediatric and adult care is a known period of disease flare and medication non-adherence. Proactive planning reduces this risk.\n\n4. **Reproductive health** (by age 14-16):\n- Methotrexate is teratogenic. Discuss contraception with adolescent females on methotrexate.\n- Pre-conception planning should include stopping methotrexate 3 months before conception.\n- Biologics have variable safety in pregnancy (discuss when appropriate).',
